From 6be3a69a56a0cbfad1687c4020b4da15a807ca77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 19:38:48 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] zed://agent?session=<id> reopens an existing thread

`zed://agent` could start a conversation but never return to one. The URL
handler accepted a single parameter, `?prompt=`, and every path through it
ended at `new_agent_thread_with_external_source_prompt` -- so a link could
seed a new thread and nothing could point at a thread that already existed.

Most of what was needed was already here, on both sides:

  * `ThreadMetadataStore::entry_by_session` resolves a session id to the
    thread's metadata row -- which records the agent that owns the thread
    and the folders it was started in -- and `load_agent_thread` opens a
    thread from those three facts. That pair is what the sidebar uses when a
    thread is clicked.

  * The id is not a Zed-local handle. It is the agent session id -- for an
    ACP agent, the same id the agent itself uses. Verified against this
    machine: two session ids out of `sidebar_threads` both have a Claude Code
    transcript of the same name under ~/.claude/projects. So whatever produces
    the link -- a share link, a shell script, a tray daemon reading agent hooks
    -- needs to know nothing about Zed's internal thread ids.

What was missing was the plumbing between them. This adds `session_id` and
`cwd` to `OpenRequestKind::AgentPanel`, teaches `parse_agent_url` to read
`session=` and `cwd=`, and routes a request carrying a session to
`AgentPanel::open_thread_by_session`, in the window that holds its project.

**The first version of this patch routed to `AgentPanel::open_thread`, in
whichever window had focus, and both halves were wrong.** Measured on
2026-09-29 with one Zed window per project on separate KDE virtual desktops:
a tray notification for a thread of project A, clicked from the desktop
showing project B, opened in B and failed with `no thread found with ID:
SessionId(...)`. Two defects, each enough on its own:

  * **The window.** `get_any_active_multi_workspace` returns the focused
    window, and a click on a notification is focused wherever it happens --
    the desktop it came from says nothing about the thread. The handler now
    resolves the thread's folders first -- from its metadata row, else from
    `cwd` -- and opens them through `workspace::open_paths`, the path `zed
    <folder>` takes: the window already holding that project is found across
    every window and raised, and one is opened only when none holds it. The
    focused window remains only for a link that carries no folders, and for a
    thread on a remote host, whose folders name paths on that host.

  * **The agent.** `open_thread` is the share-link entry and resolves every id
    to `Agent::NativeAgent`, passing no folders. For a session an external
    agent owns -- every thread the Claude adapter produces -- the native agent
    looks the id up in its own database, finds nothing, and fails; on the way,
    the metadata row is rebound to the focused workspace's folders, so the
    thread disappears from its own project's history. `open_thread_by_session`
    takes the agent and the folders off the row instead, and only falls back
    to `open_thread` when no row exists. Its test fails against `open_thread`
    with `left: NativeAgent, right: Stub`, which is the defect exactly.

The metadata store loads its rows from disk at startup, so the handler waits
on `reload_task` before reading one: a link that launches Zed would otherwise
find no row, fall through to `cwd`, and then to `open_thread`.

`cwd` is a fallback, never an override: the row records where the thread was
started, while `cwd` is where the link's producer saw the session -- for a
tray fed by Claude Code hooks, possibly a subdirectory. Only absolute paths
are accepted; a relative one would resolve against wherever Zed was started.

Two decisions worth stating, because both are silent if wrong:

  * **`session` wins over `prompt`.** A link that names a thread is asking for
    that thread; seeding a prompt into it would put words into a conversation
    the user reopened to read. They are mutually exclusive in practice, and the
    precedence has a test rather than a comment.

  * **An empty `session=` is not a session.** `?session=&prompt=hello` would
    otherwise resolve to no thread AND swallow the prompt -- the one input that
    could silently do nothing at all. It falls back to the prompt path, also
    under test.

An id this machine has never seen is not an error: it falls through to
`open_thread`, which handles the unknown-session case by loading it -- what
makes a native share link from another host work at all.

Upstreamable as-is: it adds parameters to an existing scheme, changes no
default, and every behaviour it exposes was already reachable from the
sidebar.

The handler also raises the window before touching the panel. Focusing a
panel only moves focus *inside* Zed; on a desktop with virtual workspaces
the window stays wherever it was left and the link looks like it did
nothing. `activate_window` is the same call `zed://open` already makes.
Whether the compositor honours it is its decision -- on Wayland a request
from an unfocused process is usually refused, by design -- but asking is
still correct, and on X11 it is enough on its own.
---

